---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { facts, operator, processors } from '../lib/site';

// The page the notetaker's chat notice links to (services/meetings/src/lib/notice.js
// NOTETAKER_PAGE). Its readers are mostly people in someone else's meeting, not
// AlgoMinutes users: what the notetaker is, who sent it, what it records, where
// that goes, and what they can do about it. It never claims recording is lawful
// anywhere (docs/CONSENT.md §3).
const recall = processors.find((p) => p.id === 'recall-ai')!;
---
<Base
  title="The AlgoMinutes notetaker"
  description="Why an AlgoMinutes notetaker is in your meeting, what it records, where that goes, and what you can do."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>The AlgoMinutes notetaker</h1>
    <p>
      If a participant called "<em>Name</em>'s notetaker (AlgoMinutes)" joined your meeting, someone in the meeting
      sent it: an AlgoMinutes user who wants a transcript and summary of the meeting. It joins as a visible
      participant, posts a notice in the meeting chat when it joins (and again for anyone who joins later), and
      records only while it's in the meeting.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what">What it records</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The meeting's audio, while the notetaker is in the meeting. Not video.</li>
      <li>The names participants show in the meeting, and when each person spoke, so the transcript says who said what.</li>
      <li>We don't keep email addresses or the meeting chat.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The recording becomes a note in the AlgoMinutes account of the person who sent the notetaker: a transcript, a
      summary, action items and decisions. It isn't shared with anyone else, and nobody else's account gets a copy.
    </p>

    <h2 id="control">What you can do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Remove it.</strong> The meeting's host can remove the notetaker at any time, like any participant. It then stops recording and leaves.</li>
      <li><strong>Don't let it in.</strong> In Google Meet, the notetaker waits to be admitted, like a guest. If nobody admits it, it leaves after {facts.notetakerWaitMinutes} minutes without recording anything.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask for the recording to be deleted.</strong> Ask the person who sent the notetaker: they can delete the note, and its recording, from AlgoMinutes at any time. You can also contact us at <a href={`mailto:${operator.privacy}`}>{operator.privacy}</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="where">Where the recording goes</h2>
    <p>
      The notetaker is run for us by {recall.service}, in {recall.where}. Recall.ai holds the recording only until
      AlgoMinutes has its copy, then it's deleted there; anything left is deleted automatically after {facts.notetakerRetentionHours} hours.
      AlgoMinutes then stores and processes it in Sydney, Australia, as our
      <a href="/privacy#processors">Privacy Policy</a> describes. We don't use recordings to train AI models.
    </p>

    <h2 id="consent">Consent</h2>
    <p>
      Recording laws differ between places, and many require everyone's consent. The person who sends the notetaker
      is responsible for telling the meeting and getting any consent the law requires; our
      <a href="/terms">Terms</a> require it. The notetaker's visible name and its chat notice are there so nobody is
      recorded without knowing.
    </p>

    <h2 id="contact">Questions</h2>
    <p>
      Contact us at <a href={`mailto:${operator.privacy}`}>{operator.privacy}</a>. {operator.name} runs AlgoMinutes;
      see our <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> for your rights and how to complain.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
